  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA weak upper-level cold-core low will drift slowly southward\nacross the Ohio Valley today into the Tennessee Valley tonight.\nStrongest synoptic forcing for ascent remains focused generally \nsouth and west of the forecast area. Precipitable water values \nacross the region fall in the 1.3 to 1.5 inch range, with warm \ncloud depths only around 5000 ft and equilibrium levels around \n25 to 30kft with long skinny elevated CAPE profiles. This \ngeneral mix of positive/negative factors for efficient rainfall \nproduction should help to limit convective rates to around half \nto perhaps one inch per hour rates in the strongest updrafts.\n\nHowever, steering flow remains exceptionally weak, yielding very\nslow cell motions around 5 to 10 mph. Convection that does\ndevelop will be capable of persisting over localized areas for\nseveral hours, potentially overcoming suppressed 3-hour flash \nflood guidance from antecedent rainfall. The Flash Flash Flood \nWatch has been trimmed and realigned to focus on areas across \nthe southern coalfield where several hours of moderate to heavy\nrainfall could coincide with sensitive soils. While 3-hour \nflash flood guidance portions of southeast Ohio is even more\nsuppressed, marginally over 1 inch, overall confidence in \nconvective coverage remains too low to warrant an expansion into\nthat area at this time. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs the upper low weakens and opens into a wave while moving\neastward on Thursday, lingering shower coverage over the\nmountains will gradually diminish from north to south. Surface\nhigh pressure building over Quebec and a deepening coastal low\noff the Atlantic coast will continue a persistent northeasterly\nflow across the region. This flow regime will continue to advect\na much drier continental airmass from eastern Canada into the \narea Thursday through the weekend. \n\nDewpoints will crash into the 40s by Friday afternoon, with\nrelative humidity values dropping noticeably. High temperatures\nFriday through Monday will range comfortably in the lower to mid\n70s across the lowlands and 60s in the Northeast Mountains. With\nclear skies and light winds under high pressure, optimal\nradiational cooling and cold air drainage will occur Friday\nnight and Saturday night. Overnight lows will drop into the\nupper 30s to lower 40s in sheltered mountain valleys, and the \nmid 40s to lower 50s across the lowlands. Could even see some\npatchy frost in the mountain valleys Friday morning. This will\nalso likely yield valley fog each night.\n\nTemperatures will begin a warming trend into the middle of next \nweek as southerly flow returns ahead of low pressure organizing \nin the lee of the Rockies by Monday night.",
